      "text": "### Background on the Book and the Author\n\n**Book Information:**\n- **Title:** The Deafening Silence: Non-Verbal Communication in Four Pacific Societies\n- **Author:** Dr. Mereana Tāwhiri\n- **Publication Year:** 2019\n- **Publisher:** Oceanic University Press\n- **ISBN:** 978-1-83748-291-5\n- **Summary:** This scholarly work discusses silence as a complex form of communication, arguing against the notion that silence simply signifies the absence of speech. Dr. Tāwhiri delineates a typology of silence based on 12 years of ethnographic research across Polynesian, Micronesian, and Melanesian communities. The book establishes six types of communicative silence and examines their roles in kinship, rituals, and social interactions.\n\n**Author Background:**\n- Dr. Mereana Tāwhiri is an Associate Professor of Linguistic Anthropology at the University of Auckland. She has indigenous heritage from Ngāti Porou and Sāmoan ancestry. Her PhD was obtained from the Australian National University in 2006. Her research primarily revolves around non-verbal communication and silence in Oceanic cultures. Prior to her monograph, she authored numerous peer-reviewed articles in reputable journals.\n\n### Summary of Main Arguments, Theoretical Framework, and Methodology\n\n**Main Arguments:**\nDr. Tāwhiri argues that silence functions as a structured form of communication that is deeply embedded in the social fabric of Pacific societies. It is not merely an absence of sound but a rich, communicative act with various meanings depending on context and relationship dynamics.  \n\n**Theoretical Framework:**\nThe work draws from ethnolinguistic and anthropological frameworks to analyze silence in its cultural context. It leverages concepts from kinship theory, which illustrates how silence is influenced by social relationships and cultural rituals.\n\n**Methodology:**\nThe methodology is primarily ethnographic, grounded in twelve years of fieldwork across different Pacific regions. Dr. Tāwhiri develops a six-category typology of communicative silence:\n1. **Nooanoa** - held relational silence\n2. **Faka'apa'apa** - respectful deferential silence\n3. [Additional categories not specified]\n   \nThe book underscores the importance of studying silence on its own terms and the necessity of contextual understanding in analyzing non-verbal communication.\n\n### Reception in Anthropology or Linguistics Journals\n\n1. **Review in the Journal of Linguistic Anthropology (Vol. 30, No. 1, 2020):** This journal describes Dr. Tāwhiri's work as an essential contribution to the field, noting that her categorization of silence is grounded in substantial fieldwork and well-theorized. The review praises the opening vignette from Fakarava as exceptionally compelling, suggesting the book is a significant reading for those interested in non-verbal communication in Pacific cultures.\n2. **Publisher's Perspective:** The book is described as a landmark work in Pacific anthropology, emphasizing the novel approach to understanding communicative silence in the cultural contexts of Polynesia, Micronesia, and Melanesia.\n\n### Additional Resource Links\n- [Dr. Mereana Tāwhiri — Faculty Profile, University of Auckland](https://openbooks.net/authors/mereana-tawhiri).\n- [The Deafening Silence — Oceanic University Press](https://openbooks.net/books/the-deafening-silence).\n- [Review: The Deafening Silence — Journal of Linguistic Anthropology](https://openbooks.net/reviews/the-deafening-silence).\n\nThis information should provide a comprehensive foundation for your seminar on this important work in linguistic anthropology.",
      "mediaUrl": null
    }
